\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\title{Vegetation Recovery After Wildfire from Satellite Time Series}
\author{D. Kovacs and A. Nyquist}
\date{May 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Wildfire regimes are shifting across Mediterranean climates,
and the recovery trajectory of vegetation after a burn
is an important indicator of ecosystem resilience.
We analyse a decade of moderate resolution satellite imagery
to characterise post-fire recovery across a gradient of burn severity.

\section{Study Area}
The study area covers three large fire perimeters in a Mediterranean region.
Burn severity was mapped from the differenced normalised burn ratio
using pre-fire and immediately post-fire scenes.
Field plots provide an independent validation of the severity classes.

\section{Data Preprocessing}
All scenes are atmospherically corrected with a common processor
and reprojected onto the analysis grid.
Clouds and cloud shadows are masked with the quality band,
and only observations with full coverage of a perimeter are retained.

\section{Methods}
We compute a vegetation index for every cloud free observation
and fit a piecewise linear trajectory to each pixel.
The breakpoint of the trajectory marks the transition
from rapid early recovery to the slower late phase.
Pixels are then grouped by severity class and land cover type.

\section{Results}
Recovery is fastest in low severity patches
and slowest where crown fire consumed the canopy.
\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\textwidth]{figures/ndvi_recovery_curve.pdf}
\caption{Median vegetation index trajectory by burn severity class.}
\end{figure}
The median high severity pixel takes more than five years
to return to its pre-fire index value.

\section{Uncertainty}
Trajectory fits are bootstrapped at the pixel level
to quantify the uncertainty of the estimated breakpoint.
Breakpoints in the first two years after the fire are well constrained,
while later breakpoints carry wider intervals in the high severity class.

\section{Discussion}
Repeated burns shorten the recovery window
and can push shrubland toward a grass dominated state.
Management that reduces fuel continuity in young stands
has the largest effect on long-term recovery.

\section{Conclusion}
Satellite time series provide a scalable way to monitor recovery
and to flag areas where intervention is most likely to succeed.
\end{document}

